<template>
    <SiteShell page="features">
        <div class="page-head">
            <h1>Features</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                Three lines share one design document: capture, review and
                delivery. Nothing here is assumed from a file extension, and the
                capability matrix is the only claim that counts.
            </p>
        </div>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Contracts</h2>
                <p>The pieces every surface shares, with the implemented subset stated explicitly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cards">
                <article class="contract">
                    <span class="chip chip--ok">available</span>
                    <h3>Handles</h3>
                    <p>
                        Creating or opening a design returns a handle. Every later
                        call names that handle, so the studio, CLI and SDK never
                        guess which document an operation belongs to.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="contract">
                    <span class="chip chip--ok">available</span>
                    <h3>Revisions</h3>
                    <p>
                        Every successful edit increments the design revision, and
                        save and export report the revision they used. Multi-writer
                        revision conflict detection is not implemented yet.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="contract">
                    <span class="chip chip--ok">available</span>
                    <h3>Error codes</h3>
                    <p>
                        Errors are namespaced and stable. A supported operation
                        succeeds or reports a specific code; an unimplemented
                        capability reports <code>capability/not-implemented</code>
                        rather than a fabricated result.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="contract">
                    <span class="chip chip--ok">available</span>
                    <h3>Capability matrix</h3>
                    <p>
                        Engine, project operations, formats, checks, renderers and
                        export targets each report their own status. Unsupported is
                        a first-class answer.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Capture</h2>
                <span class="chip chip--ok">working</span>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Sheets, components, nets and wires live in one <code>.oeda</code> document.</li>
                <li>Every sheet and object keeps a deterministic identity and the design keeps a revision.</li>
                <li>A failed edit is rejected before it changes the document.</li>
                <li>Undo and redo keep the newest 32 snapshots per open handle; a new edit clears the redo stack.</li>
                <li>Sheets can be added, renamed and deleted while empty; the last sheet is protected.</li>
                <li>Components can be moved, updated and deleted; deleting one removes its net nodes and detaches wires.</li>
                <li>Net labels are first-class annotations with <code>local</code>, <code>global</code> or <code>hierarchical</code> scope, editable by id; labels never create or merge declared nets.</li>
                <li>Junctions, no-connect flags, text and labels can be listed without editing; <code>stats</code> reports deterministic per-sheet and design-wide counts.</li>
                <li>Save writes a temporary file and renames it, so a partial document is never left behind.</li>
                <li>History lives in memory per handle in the SDK and studio. The CLI keeps the same bounded stacks in a <code>&lt;file&gt;.history.json</code> sidecar and never writes history into the <code>.oeda</code> file.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Review</h2>
                <span class="chip chip--ok">basic</span>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>ERC reports duplicate references, duplicate net names and net nodes that point at missing components or pins.</li>
                <li>ERC warns about pins that no net connects.</li>
                <li>The nets check reports floating wires, wires that name an undeclared net, single-node nets and empty sheets.</li>
                <li>The footprints check reports components without a value.</li>
                <li>The connectivity report also counts net labels per sheet by scope; <code>stats</code> reports every object count, BOM rows and buses deterministically.</li>
                <li>Every finding carries its code, severity, sheet, object and revision; only errors fail the report.</li>
                <li>DRC, clearance and power are reported as unsupported and never as passing.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Delivery</h2>
                <span class="chip chip--ok">netlist, SVG, BOM and PDF</span>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>The netlist writer emits <code># OpenEDA netlist v1</code>, then <code>NET</code> and <code>NODE</code> lines.</li>
                <li>The SVG writer draws a sheet frame, wires and component boxes with reference and value labels.</li>
                <li>The BOM writer groups components by value and emits <code>Comment,Designator,Quantity</code> with sorted designators.</li>
                <li>The PDF writer emits a one-page A4 landscape vector document with a cross-reference table, sheet names, component boxes and wires.</li>
                <li>Every projection is written atomically from the committed revision and reports its byte count and a small artifact summary.</li>
                <li>KiCad schematic and board, Gerber and drill remain declared targets that reject with <code>capability/not-implemented</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Command surface</h2>
                <p>What each entry point actually does today against the native model.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table class="table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Surface</th>
                            <th>What exists today</th>
                            <th>Status</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="cell-mono">CLI</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>capabilities</code>, <code>create</code>,
                                <code>open</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>inspect</code>
                                and <code>export</code> work; <code>undo</code>,
                                <code>redo</code>, <code>component add/move/update/delete</code>,
                                <code>net connect/rename</code>, <code>wire delete</code>,
                                <code>label add/update/delete</code> and
                                <code>sheet add/rename/delete</code> edit a document in place;
                                <code>junction list</code>, <code>no-connect list</code>,
                                <code>label list</code>, <code>sheet list</code> and
                                <code>stats</code> are read-only.
                                Unsupported checks and targets reject.
                            </td>
                            <td><span class="chip chip--ok">document core</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="cell-mono">SDK</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>@wae/open-eda</code> exposes create/open, editing with
                                undo/redo, sheet and object edits, inspection, save and
                                netlist/SVG/BOM/PDF export with typed errors. The
                                <code>Design</code> handle is wired to the native model.
                            </td>
                            <td><span class="chip chip--ok">document core</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="cell-mono">Studio</td>
                            <td>
                                The VMZ shell edits through the native host over
                                JSON-RPC and shows documents, sheets with add/rename/delete,
                                objects, undo/redo state, checks, findings and export targets
                                including BOM and PDF. The canvas viewport and its tools are
                                still disabled.
                            </td>
                            <td><span class="chip chip--warn">shell plus document</span></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="cell-mono">Skills</td>
                            <td>
                                <code>@wae/open-eda-skills</code> describes the
                                executable CLI commands, their inputs and the
                                capabilities that still reject.
                            </td>
                            <td><span class="chip chip--ok">usable</span></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Format honesty</h2>
                <span class="chip chip--idle">15 declared</span>
            </div>
            <p>
                The format registry classifies KiCad, generic schematic and board,
                netlist, EDIF, SPICE, Gerber, drill, STEP, SVG, PDF, PNG and BOM files.
                Classification is not a support claim: an extension may be listed
                while the capability matrix still reports it as
                <code>unsupported</code>. BOM and PDF are export-only; neither can be
                imported or edited.
            </p>
            <p class="formats-cell cell-mono">
                kicad_sch · kicad_pcb · sch · brd · net · edif · spice · cir · gbr · drl · step · svg · pdf · png · bom
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="section-head">
                <h2>Current state</h2>
                <span class="chip chip--idle">no public release</span>
            </div>
            <p>
                The native model implements a schematic document core: create and
                open <code>.oeda</code>, edit sheets, components, nets and wires with
                bounded undo/redo, run ERC, net and footprint checks, and write
                netlist, SVG, BOM or PDF. The interactive renderer, board layout,
                DRC/clearance/power and KiCad, Gerber and drill exchange report
                <code>capability/not-implemented</code>. This page describes that
                surface, not a finished product.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="cta-band">
            <div>
                <span class="eyebrow">Order of work</span>
                <h2>See what is done and what is next.</h2>
